// CLOSED 2026-09-23 (squeue #302): an `@` arm over an or of variants skipped every non-scalar alternative, so its test was false and control fell to `unreachable`; such alternatives are now tested with pat_test, and the arm is fanned out per alternative.
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row at_binding_or_subpattern_enum_segfaults_run (tier 1, run 139). An `@` BINDER
// WHOSE SUB-PATTERN IS AN OR OF VARIANTS SEGFAULTS — and NOTHING is read through the binder.
//
// `match e { y @ (E::A(_) | E::B(_)) => { return 5i64; } }` over a by-value enum: rustc 1.98.1
// --edition 2024 compiles and runs, k=5, exit 0. TODAY rc 139 on base AND on the 2026-09-16f-atbind
// landing alike. Found 2026-09-16f (hand program b07).
// ⚠ NOT the binder-shape fact that round closed, and its TWO CONTROLS say so, both measured on both
// binaries: b13, the SAME or-pattern with NO `@` (`E::A(_) | E::B(_) => 5`) — correct, k=5; and b14,
// an `@` binder over the same enum with a NON-Or sub (`y @ E::B(_)`) — correct, k=5. So neither `@`
// alone nor the or-pattern alone is the fact: it is the COMBINATION, and it is decided in the arm's
// alternative dispatch, not where the binder is given storage. Rowed with reason 1 (the fact has no
// carrier at the binder site: that site binds correctly on the landed binary and this still crashes).
package at_binding_or_subpattern_enum_segfaults_run;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c * 10i64 + self.v; } } }
fn rd(p: *mut i64) -> i64 { return unsafe { *p }; }
enum E { A(i64), B(i64) }
fn g() -> i64 {
    let e: E = E::B(7i64);
    match e {
        y @ (E::A(_) | E::B(_)) => { return 5i64; }
    }
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let k: i64 = g();
    unsafe { printf("k=%ld\n".as_ptr(), k); }
    if k != 5i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
